          "citation": "chapter 3 p. 49"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "ii-chord-enclosure-with-diatonic-above-chromatic-below",
      "narrative": "For min7 chord tones, Larsen prescribes: 'the lick played over the Gm7 encloses the chord tone with a diatonic note above and a chromatic note below. The same idea is applied first to the root, then the 3rd.' This two-note enclosure formula is applicable to any chord tone on the min7. Chapter 3, p. 47.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:chromatic-enclosure-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 3 p. 47"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "ii-chord-coltrane-minor-pattern",
      "narrative": "For min7, Larsen prescribes the Coltrane 1-b3-4-5 variant: 'Coltrane patterns (1 2 35 on a major chord, 1 b3 4.5 on a minor chord)' -- on a minor chord use the 1-b3-4-5 grouping to break up scalic lines and create interest over fast-tempo changes. Chapter 1, p. 24.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:melodic-line-construction"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 1 p. 24"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "i",
      "granularity_name": "other"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "chord-tone-omission-for-open-sound",
      "narrative": "For min7, Larsen sanctions omitting the 3rd to open the sound: 'I have omitted the 3rd (Bb) of Gm7 which immediately makes the line more open sounding. In isolation, it could sound like a G7sus4 chord, but is fine when we hear it in the context of our key.' The rule is contextual tolerance -- the overall harmonic frame justifies the omission. Chapter 3, p. 52.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:harmonic-substitution-lattice"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 3 p. 52"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "c",
      "granularity_name": "omission-priority"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "tonic-minor-ebmaj7-inversion-skip",
      "narrative": "For min7 as tonic (Cm7), Larsen prescribes an Ebmaj7 arpeggio inversion-skip: 'the line over Cm7 has an Ebmaj7 arpeggio that begins on the root, but skips down to the G, then ascends the arpeggio ... This type of phrase is very common with George Benson and Grant Green.' Chapter 6, p. 77.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:harmonic-substitution-lattice"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 6 p. 77"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "i",
      "granularity_name": "other"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "tonic-minor-quintal-arpeggio",
      "narrative": "For min7 as tonic (Cm7), Larsen prescribes quintal arpeggios: 'The quintal arpeggio is also a great open sounding structure to apply to a Cm7 chord. Here it is used from the Eb and spells out a Cm11 sound. The quintal arpeggio is preceded by a diatonic enclosure of the Eb.' Chapter 6, p. 78.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:harmonic-substitution-lattice"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
