          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 6 p. 78"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "i",
      "granularity_name": "other"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "v7-m7b5-on-3rd-substitution",
      "narrative": "For dom7, Larsen's primary prescription is the m7b5-on-the-3rd substitution: 'playing a m7b5 chord on the 3rd of a dominant 7 chord causes us to avoid playing the root (G) and adds the rich-sounding 9th (A) ... It sounds so good that many musicians always use it instead of the original arpeggio.' This is 'one of the most common uses of arpeggios in jazz.' Chapter 1, p. 15.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:harmonic-substitution-lattice"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 1 p. 15"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "v7-upper-structure-arpeggio-hierarchy",
      "narrative": "For dom7, Larsen prescribes a strict hierarchy for upper-structure arpeggio roots: 'Jazz musicians will often play a new arpeggio from any note in the underlying chord ... arpeggios from the third (B) fifth (D) or even the seventh (F).' The 3rd-rooted arpeggio is most versatile; as you move to the 5th and 7th, 'you include fewer notes from the original chord, so you need to be a bit more careful.' Chapter 1, p. 14-15.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:harmonic-substitution-lattice"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 1 p. 14-15"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "v7-sus4-avoidance",
      "narrative": "For dom7, Larsen warns: 'The only thing to watch for here is to choose carefully when to use the C note. Playing a C over a G7 chord will suggest a G7sus4 sound. This might be desirable in some instances but not in others.' The 11th must be deployed with deliberate intent, not by default. Chapter 1, p. 23.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:harmonic-substitution-lattice"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 1 p. 23"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "polarity": "proscriptive",
      "cross_ref": [
        "dom7-sus4-intentional-deployment"
      ],
      "granularity_level": "d",
      "granularity_name": "color-tone-policy"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "v7-diminished-scale-upper-structure-triads",
      "narrative": "For dom7, Larsen prescribes using the half-whole diminished scale's four embedded major triads (F, Ab, B, D) as upper-structure melodic material: 'The most common sound associated with this scale is probably the 13b9. Guitarists generally think of this as an upper-structure triad placed above a dominant 7th chord. E.g. F713b9 is a D major triad over an F7 chord.' Chapter 5, p. 66-67.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:diminished-symmetry-triad-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 5 p. 66-67"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "d",
      "granularity_name": "color-tone-policy"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "v7-diminished-triad-pair-ab-d-for-extensions",
      "narrative": "For dom7, Larsen prescribes the Ab+D major triad pair to express 13b9 color: 'The combination of the Ab and D major triads conveys the sound of an F7 with a #9 (Ab), b9 (Gb) and 13 (D). The melody between the Ab and the D creates a voice leading transition between the two, as the Ab moves to A and the Eb moves to D.' Chapter 5, p. 70.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:diminished-symmetry-triad-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 5 p. 70"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "d",
      "granularity_name": "color-tone-policy"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "v7-root-triad-omission",
      "narrative": "For dom7, Larsen prescribes omitting the root-position major triad in diminished-scale melodic lines: 'I don't often use the F major triad because it doesn't add any colour.' The four diminished-scale triads over F7 reduce in practice to three (Ab, B, D), with F itself excluded. Chapter 6, p. 74.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:diminished-symmetry-triad-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 6 p. 74"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "polarity": "proscriptive",
      "granularity_level": "c",
      "granularity_name": "omission-priority"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "v7-triad-pair-f-b-for-11-b9",
      "narrative": "For dom7 (F7), Larsen prescribes first-inversion F major and B major triads in combination to highlight #11 and b9 extensions: 'The F7 line uses first inversion F major and B major triads. This combination highlights the #11 and b9 extensions.' Chapter 6, p. 75.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:diminished-symmetry-triad-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 6 p. 75"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "d",
      "granularity_name": "color-tone-policy"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "v7-triplet-7th-arpeggio-bebop",
      "narrative": "For dom7, Larsen prescribes triplet rhythms on 7th arpeggios as a bebop device: 'Using triplets to play 7th arpeggios is a great bebop trick that works well in a more modern jazz context as well.' Rhythmic variation via eighth-note triplets embellishes arpeggios and implies unwritten passing chords. Chapter 4, p. 56.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:melodic-line-construction"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p. 56"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "i",
      "granularity_name": "other"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "v7-suggest-unwritten-passing-chords",
      "narrative": "For dom7 (Bb7alt), Larsen prescribes implying unwritten passing chords to create forward motion: 'Suggesting passing chords that aren't written is a great way to create motion towards a chord change ... the Gm7 arpeggio ... creates a strong motion from the Fm7 to Bb7alt chords.' Chapter 4, p. 58.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:melodic-line-construction"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p. 58"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "v7-harmonic-minor-dominant-pull",
      "narrative": "For dom7 as V, Larsen prescribes applying harmonic minor material to create a pull to the next chord: 'Uses the harmonic minor on Bb7 to pull towards the Eb7 (in this case playing the pattern of a Ddim arpeggio).' Harmonic minor over the dominant signals the tonic resolution. Chapter 6, p. 82.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:melodic-line-construction"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 6 p. 82"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7alt",
      "function_role": "altered-dominant-guide-tone-arpeggio-selection",
      "narrative": "For dom7alt, Larsen's primary prescription is guide-tone arpeggio selection: 'The defining tones of a Bb7alt chord are the 3rd and 7th (F and Ab), so if we use arpeggios from the Bb Altered scale that contain those intervals we can stay close to the dominant sound and introduce some cool altered extensions. Only two chords contain both notes: E7 and G#m7b5.' Chapter 4, p. 54.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:harmonic-substitution-lattice"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p. 54"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7alt",
      "function_role": "altered-dominant-m7b5-on-3rd-best-candidate",
      "narrative": "For dom7alt, Larsen prescribes G#m7b5 (m7b5 on the 3rd of the tritone sub) as the best arpeggio: 'G#m7b5 is related to E7, as it can be viewed as an arpeggio built on the 3rd of E7. Of the two options, the G#m7b5 arpeggio is probably the best candidate to spell out a Bb7alt sound.' Its interval map over Bb7: b7(G#/Ab), b9(B), 3(D), b13(F#). Chapter 4, p. 54.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:harmonic-substitution-lattice"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p. 54"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7alt",
      "function_role": "altered-dominant-tritone-sub-relationship",
      "narrative": "For dom7alt, Larsen prescribes framing the altered scale through the tritone substitute relationship: 'The Bb Altered scale is the seventh mode of B Melodic Minor ... E7 is the tritone substitute of Bb7.' This reframing makes arpeggios from B Melodic Minor directly available over Bb7alt. Chapter 4, p. 53-54.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jens-larsen:modern-jazz-guitar-concepts:harmonic-substitution-lattice"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "modern-jazz-guitar-concepts",
